<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- CBS copy of epsilon_crane_behavior_tree/behavior_trees/subtree_a2b_movement.xml.

     Structurally the same tree: approval wraps activate, execute, deactivate, and the
     Fallback's ForceFailure branch deactivates again on any failure inside it. The only CBS
     difference is which child executes the move (see ONE COMMANDER below).

     CONTROLLER OWNERSHIP. This subtree used to drop every SwitchController node, on the
     grounds that `crane_supervisor` was the sole caller of
     /controller_manager/switch_controller and a STRICT switch that found the world already
     changed under it would fault. That owner is obsolete and no launch in this package starts
     it, so nothing activated `trajectory_controller_a2b` at all: the spawner brings it up
     inactive (blockpose/launch_context/gazebo_model_bt.launch.py), the JTC only reads
     ~/joint_trajectory between on_activate and on_deactivate, and on `cbs_mpc_active` the
     MPC's horizon is remapped onto that topic, so the horizon reached an inactive controller
     and the machine never moved. The tree owns the switch again, exactly as the timber one
     does.

     That ownership is also the deadman. GetUserApproval gates the activation, so before the
     operator confirms there is no active controller for the horizon to reach, and on failure
     the ForceFailure branch deactivates. Approval gating the *activation* is what makes it
     gate the motion on `cbs_mpc_active`, where the planner's own publication of
     /crane/reference (not a goal this tree sends) is what starts the MPC driving.

     APPROVAL IS HELD, AND IT IS NOT HARDWARE-ONLY. GetUserApproval is
     RemoteCtrlStates::button12 *held*: GetMsgCondition re-reads the last message every tick
     and halts the child on a false or on 5 ticks without a message, so releasing mid-move
     drops into the ForceFailure branch and deactivates. The producer is not the gpio
     controller alone: timber_crane_tui's `remote_ctrl` publishes
     /gpio_controller/remote_ctrl_states straight from the keyboard, and the CBS sim launches
     already start it under gui:=True. So in sim the operator holds `z` in the TUI xterm for
     the whole move (up to WaitForMoveComplete's 120 s); with gui:=False nothing publishes the
     topic and every approval node in this tree blocks.

     STRICTNESS. The switches leave `strictness` unset, so the node's BEST_EFFORT default
     applies: controller_manager warns "already active" and drops that controller from the
     request instead of failing, which is what the old comment feared from a STRICT switch.
     Every launch that runs this tree spawns trajectory_controller_a2b inactive anyway;
     the spawners that bring it up active are the mp and hardware ones, which run no tree.

     ONE COMMANDER PER MOVE (issue 107). `trajectory_controller_a2b` takes a trajectory two
     ways: the action goal below, and `~/joint_trajectory`, whose documented behaviour is
     "always replace old msg with new one". A live goal does not close the topic path, because
     JTC sets `subscriber_is_active_` in on_activate/on_deactivate only, so an MPC re-goaling
     by topic every T_s would run against this goal's `goal_time` and per-joint tolerances.
     146 landed that remap, so issue 148 closes this: exactly one of the two children runs per
     move, and which one is bound at launch, not in this file. WaitForMoveComplete reads the
     `a2b_mpc_drives` ROS parameter (true only on controller:=cbs_mpc_active), writes it to
     `mpc_drives`, and returns SKIPPED when it is false, so on `pid`, `mpc` and `cbs_mpc` the
     Sequence falls through to the unchanged action.

     WaitForMoveComplete is the completion oracle the action goal used to be. It is not a
     like-for-like replacement: it also requires /crane/sway_settled to read SETTLED_YES,
     which no goal ever checked. See the node's header. The action arm gets that half back
     from a second instance with `settle_only` after the goal (issue 220), so both arms
     leave the load still, and both therefore need crane_mpc alive to publish the verdict,
     in shadow mode if it is not driving.

     epsilon_crane_behavior_tree is a shared reference package and is not edited. -->
<root BTCPP_format="4">
  <!-- The one MPC-execute subtree: A2B moves, lift, carrying descent. Caller sets:
         ex_goal_tolerance     arrival tolerance [rad|m]
         ex_sway_bound         sway rate bound at arrival [rad/s], 0: the MPC verdict's
         ex_hold_s             dwell under the arrival bounds [s]: 2.7 (one period of the
                               carried pendulum) loaded, 0.3 empty; issue 255. Set it
                               in every caller, not only where it is short: the blackboard
                               is shared across the whole run, so an unset key would leave
                               a placement paying the empty move's dwell.
         ex_publish_reference  true: hand {trajectory} to the MPC (grip plans); false where
                               crane_planning already published it (A2B)
         ex_until_landed       true: done on landing (WaitForLoadOff), not on arrival. That
                               gate also reads place_g, the K8 -> block-centre offset it puts
                               the block's height at.
         ex_watch_landed       true: a loaded descent to a waypoint. The same gate runs as a
                               watch beside the arrival wait and *fails* the move on any
                               load-off, which there is early ground contact. Exclusive with
                               ex_until_landed; set both in every caller, the blackboard is
                               shared and a stale `true` would watch an empty lift.
       Writes load_off: did the load come off, however the gate ended. The caller reads it to
       tell early contact from any other failure (issue 289).
       mpc_drives is written by WaitForMoveComplete; a landing descent skips that node and
       reads the value of the move before. -->
  <BehaviorTree ID="SubTreeCbsA2BMovement">
    <Fallback name="Fallback to deactivate controllers">
      <Decorator ID="GetUserApproval" topic="/gpio_controller/remote_ctrl_states"
        wait_rising_edge="False">
        <Sequence>
          <SwitchController name="Activate controller"
            activate_controllers="trajectory_controller_a2b"
            deactivate_controllers=""
            server_timeout="1000"
            service_name="/controller_manager/switch_controller"/>
          <!-- `joints`: the actuated five. The /a2b_movement answer is the canonical eight and
               carries theta6_tip_joint / theta7_tilt_joint, which the plan does not aim and
               /crane/reference does not track; q9_left_rail_joint has goal tolerance 0.00, i.e.
               the controller does not check it either.
               Landing: the move branch never ends the move. It publishes and sleeps (or runs
               the goal, whose end or abort decides nothing: one run pushed the block 1 cm into
               the ground and the JTC aborted on the slew it dragged); WaitForLoadOff ends it on
               landing and fails it past the kinematic floor or on timeout. -->
          <Parallel success_count="1" failure_count="1">
            <Sequence>
              <!-- Landing: hold still while WaitForLoadOff takes its 500 ms reference;
                   taken during the descent's start dip it read the block ~20 % light.
                   The early-contact watch takes the same reference, so it waits too. -->
              <Sleep msec="600" _skipIf="!ex_until_landed &amp;&amp; !ex_watch_landed"/>
              <!-- Nested: a script reads both sides of ||, and mpc_drives is unset until
                   the first move's WaitForMoveComplete. Grip moves follow a move_above. -->
              <Sequence _skipIf="!ex_publish_reference">
                <Action ID="PublishMpcReference" trajectory="{trajectory}"
                  _skipIf="!mpc_drives"/>
              </Sequence>
              <!-- 120 s is the stalled-clock backstop, not the window (issue 239). What
                   bounds this wait is crane_mpc's own hold: the node watches
                   /crane/mpc/horizon and fails the move the moment the MPC stops driving
                   it, which is `max_reference_age` (20 s) past the end of the plan on
                   /crane/reference. Past that the JTC holds the last horizon point and
                   neither gate can change, so the 120 s was spent on a question nothing
                   would answer: measured 90 s of it, on every move, in all four runs of
                   issue 238. The value stays, because it is reached only if /clock stalls and
                   lowering it would cap the plan durations this tree does not own. -->
              <WaitForMoveComplete trajectory="{trajectory}" _skipIf="ex_until_landed"
                joints="theta1_slewing_joint,theta2_boom_joint,theta3_arm_joint,q4_big_telescope,theta8_rotator_joint"
                goal_tolerance="{ex_goal_tolerance}"
                sway_bound="{ex_sway_bound}"
                hold_s="{ex_hold_s}"
                timeout_ms="120000"
                mpc_drives="{mpc_drives}"/>
              <Fallback _skipIf="mpc_drives">
                <Action ID="FollowJointTrajectory"
                  server_name="/trajectory_controller_a2b/follow_joint_trajectory"
                  server_timeout="500"
                  trajectory="{trajectory}"
                  goal_tolerance="{ex_goal_tolerance}"
                  goal_tolerance_joints="theta1_slewing_joint,theta2_boom_joint,theta3_arm_joint,q4_big_telescope,theta8_rotator_joint"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess _skipIf="!ex_until_landed"/>
              </Fallback>
              <Sleep msec="600000" _skipIf="!ex_until_landed"/>
            </Sequence>
            <!-- 0.8: at 0.5 the jaws opened with ~375 kg still on the hook and the gripper
                 whipped off the placed block (issue 263); at 0.25 the boom bounced 0.3 m.
                 Not 0.9: the 10 s touchdown delivers ~91 %, so 0.9 risks the timeout. -->
            <!-- 150 s, was 40: the angled path's final segment is a ~10 s plan, but the
                 simple path's touchdown is the whole 1.0 m stroke (issue 289) and measured
                 ~30 s of it in sim at slow_down 1, so the 40 s was already inside a lab
                 day's pace of 2 or 3. Only reached when the block neither lands nor
                 reaches the floor, i.e. a descent that stopped in the air. -->
            <!-- place_g, not s_log_8_z: the floor is |offset| under K8, and the offset the
                 pick measured is 0.11-0.19 m shorter than the loaded bend at the slot (lab
                 day 2026-11-04), so the floor sat that much deeper. place_g is the angled
                 path's own measurement where the path takes one - the hover's, refreshed at
                 the waypoint (issue 291) - and the pick's offset where it does not; every
                 touchdown caller sets it (issue 290). -->
            <!-- floor 0.25, not the node's 1 cm default, and that is the other half of the
                 offset change above. The hover shift makes the computed centre start at
                 exactly place_z + 0.05 once the offset is the slot's own, so a 1 cm floor
                 fires about 1 cm past contact - before the 0.8 drop can confirm, which
                 takes a push ("10 cm low still left the hook carrying ~60 %", below). The
                 pick's offset read the block 0.11-0.19 m high, i.e. the floor really sat at
                 0.12-0.20 m; 0.25 keeps that budget with a correct height and stays below
                 the 0.20 m the touchdown commands, so a fully executed stroke does not trip
                 it. Deeper than the push the drop needs is what this number has to be;
                 measure the unload curve to set it properly. -->
            <Action ID="WaitForLoadOff" _skipIf="!ex_until_landed" drop_fraction="0.8"
              timeout_ms="150000" load_off="{load_off}" floor_below_target="0.25"
              mass="{payload_mass}" target_z="{place_z}"
              s_log_8_x="{s_log_8_x}" s_log_8_y="{s_log_8_y}" s_log_8_z="{place_g}"/>
            <!-- The same gate as a watch beside the arrival wait (issue 289): on a loaded
                 descent to a waypoint every load-off is early ground contact, so
                 max_above_target -1.0, i.e. no height here is acceptable, turns it into the
                 failure that ends the descent, and the caller decides release or lift-clear
                 off {load_off}. Same 0.8: on early contact the jaws may still open, so the
                 263 reason for it holds. No deadline of its own, the arrival wait is the
                 move's; 600 s is the stalled-clock backstop the landing Sleep also uses.
                 floor_below_target off (10 m, i.e. unreachable): the floor is the
                 touchdown's backstop and it is measured from place_z, while these descents
                 target place_z + 0.05 (finish, set-down) or + 0.30 (approach). At the
                 default 1 cm the lab day's own 0.3-0.5 m downward overrun trips it first,
                 and it fails with load_off false, i.e. as a plain descent failure, which
                 RetryUntilSuccessful answers by descending into the ground a second time.
                 A waypoint descent that overran without the load coming off is the arrival
                 check's business (issue 287), not this gate's. -->
            <Action ID="WaitForLoadOff" name="Watch for early contact"
              _skipIf="!ex_watch_landed" drop_fraction="0.8"
              timeout_ms="600000" max_above_target="-1.0" floor_below_target="10.0"
              load_off="{load_off}"
              mass="{payload_mass}" target_z="{place_z}"
              s_log_8_x="{s_log_8_x}" s_log_8_y="{s_log_8_y}" s_log_8_z="{s_log_8_z}"/>
          </Parallel>
          <SwitchController name="Deactivate controller"
            activate_controllers=""
            deactivate_controllers="trajectory_controller_a2b"
            server_timeout="1000"
            service_name="/controller_manager/switch_controller"/>
          <!-- The settle half the goal never had (issue 220). The goal tests positions
               and a goal_time; nothing on this arm damps sway, so a tree that moved on
               from the goal descended into a swinging block. Same node, same
               /crane/sway_settled hold, `settle_only` dropping the profile gate and the
               plan-duration wait the goal already served. Not on a landing descent:
               WaitForLoadOff ends those and the load is on the ground.
               crane_mpc is the only publisher of the verdict, so `controller:=pid`
               alone cannot pass this; wiki/commissioning.md 8a.
               Nested for the same reason PublishMpcReference above is: a single script
               reading `mpc_drives || ex_until_landed` reads both sides, and on a landing
               descent the first WaitForMoveComplete is skipped, so mpc_drives can be
               unset. The outer skip reads only what every caller sets.

               AFTER THE DEACTIVATE, NOT INSIDE THE MOVE (issue 234). Held, the loaded
               0.35 Hz tip mode does not decay at all: 60 s of hold with the A2B JTC
               active grew the swing 0.181 to 0.204 rad peak-to-peak, with the actuated
               commands ringing at the tip's own frequency (arm 0.004 rad/s at
               0.350 Hz). Deactivated, the same mode decays monotonically with
               tau = 21 s. The position hold is a pump, not a brake, so the gate has to
               be waited out with the controller off. The machine already stands with
               this controller inactive between every pair of moves, so this adds no
               state it does not already see; a failure here leaves it inactive too,
               which is where the ForceFailure branch would have put it.
               60 s, was 30: the 30 s was sized on an empty hook's ~5 s decay and a
               loaded mode has no such decay. Measured on the slowed lift of
               subtree_lift.xml, the gate is reached at ~33 s (arrival 0.092 rad/s,
               0.05 rad/s bound, 0.0522 rad/s still left at 30 s and the 2.7 s dwell
               on top), so 30 s missed it by a hair. 60 s is twice the measured
               time to the bound. A settled arrival still leaves in ~3 s, which
               is what every empty move does, so this is a bound on the bad case and
               not a dwell. -->
          <Sequence _skipIf="ex_until_landed">
            <WaitForMoveComplete name="Wait for the load to settle" settle_only="true"
              _skipIf="mpc_drives"
              trajectory="{trajectory}"
              joints="theta1_slewing_joint,theta2_boom_joint,theta3_arm_joint,q4_big_telescope,theta8_rotator_joint"
              goal_tolerance="{ex_goal_tolerance}"
              sway_bound="{ex_sway_bound}"
              hold_s="{ex_hold_s}"
              timeout_ms="60000"
              mpc_drives="{mpc_drives}"/>
          </Sequence>
        </Sequence>
      </Decorator>
      <Decorator ID="ForceFailure">
        <SwitchController name="Deactivate controller"
          activate_controllers=""
          deactivate_controllers="trajectory_controller_a2b"
          server_timeout="1000"
          service_name="/controller_manager/switch_controller"/>
      </Decorator>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
